{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>When a man has a stubborn and rebellious son.</b> The Sages said (Sanhedrin 71a): A stubborn and rebellious son never was and never will be. And why was it written in the Torah? Rather, expound and receive reward. Even so, the obligation of explanation falls upon us: why did the Torah write something that does not exist at all? One must also ponder what is stated here: ״וְכָל יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁמְעוּ וְיִרָאוּ״ — more than in other particular commandments. And further, why is it not stated here ״וְלֹא יְזִידוּן עוֹד״ as is written in parashat Shofetim (Deuteronomy 17:13)? And perhaps the reason is that since a stubborn and rebellious son never was and never will be, the parashah was not written except so that the sons would hear and fear and not do such a thing. And what is written, ״וְכָל יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁמְעוּ״, means that they would hear this parashah and the judgment written in it, and the sons would fear to defy the honored eyes of father and mother — and therefore ״וְלֹא יְזִידוּן עוֹד״ was not stated. For the expression עוֹד implies something that was already done, that it should not be done again, and this is not so — for a stubborn and rebellious son never existed from time immemorial, and this parashah was not written except to cast dread and fear upon the sons." "But it is difficult for me: ״וְכָל יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁמְעוּ״ — it should have said ״וְכָל הַבָּנִים״, for there is no need for this fear except for the sons. And in the Book of the Zohar this parashah was expounded upon all of Israel, but I say there is no need for this, for even from the plain sense of the parashah there is a wondrous moral lesson for all Israel, who are called sons of the living El. One must fear that they might rely on this and say: since we are His sons, then certainly if they are rebellious sons He will not look upon iniquity in Jacob, and He will have mercy on them as a father has mercy on sons, and He will pardon them in the manner that a father pardons his son, and they say the Holy One, blessed be He, is forgiving. And so Moses said: ״בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ (Deuteronomy 32:5) — meaning, that He called them His sons is itself their blemish, for they relied on this and committed transgression upon transgression, and trusted in their Father that He would not hand them over to the attribute of justice. Therefore the Torah wrote that the judgment is true justice — that even the father is obligated to bring his son to court and hand him over to execution, and through this all Israel would hear this parashah and fear HaShem, and not rely on the fact that He called them sons — for even in an earthly court the father is obligated to hand over his son to justice, and so too is the judgment in the heavenly court."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase וְלֹא יְזִידוּן עוֹד appears at Deuteronomy 17:13 (סנהדרין) in the context of the rebellious elder, not the stubborn and rebellious son; the Kli Yakar's citation address is accurate.", 1 "The Kli Yakar reads בָּנָיו מוּמָם in Deuteronomy 32:5 as a phrase meaning 'His sons — this is their blemish,' splitting the words against their syntactic arrangement in the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:13.987549769Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}